.Dd Jul 13, 2026
.Dt __OS_CRYPTO 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm __os_crypto_sha256 ,
.Nm __os_crypto_hmac_sha256 ,
.Nm __os_crypto_aes_gcm_encrypt ,
.Nm __os_crypto_aes_gcm_decrypt ,
.Nm __os_csprng_init ,
.Nm __os_csprng_bytes ,
.Nm __os_csprng_destroy
.Nd cryptography building blocks (hashing, AEAD, CSPRNG)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In os_crypto.h
.Ft int
.Fn __os_crypto_sha256 "const void *data" "size_t len" "uint8_t out[32]"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_crypto_hmac_sha256 "const void *key" "size_t keylen" "const void *data" "size_t len" "uint8_t out[32]"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_crypto_aes_gcm_encrypt "const uint8_t key[32]" "const uint8_t iv[12]" "const void *aad" "size_t aadlen" "const void *plaintext" "size_t len" "void *ciphertext_out" "uint8_t tag_out[16]"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_crypto_aes_gcm_decrypt "const uint8_t key[32]" "const uint8_t iv[12]" "const void *aad" "size_t aadlen" "const void *ciphertext" "size_t len" "const uint8_t tag[16]" "void *plaintext_out"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_csprng_init "__os_csprng_t **out"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_csprng_bytes "__os_csprng_t *rng" "void *buf" "size_t len"
.Ft void
.Fn __os_csprng_destroy "__os_csprng_t *rng"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm __os_crypto_*
and
.Nm __os_csprng_*
are the L0 cryptography building blocks (PLAN.md 19.2), scoped to the
two stated v1 use cases: checksums/hashing for WAL integrity, and a
per-loop cryptographically secure pseudorandom number generator
(CSPRNG).  This is a
.Em library-internal
primitive; there is no public
.Nm xtc_*
wrapper in this pass, because no consumer needs direct access yet.
.Pp
The implementation is backed
.Em only
by OpenSSL, guarded by the same preprocessor macro
.Dv XTC_TLS_BACKEND_OPENSSL
that
.Xr xtc_tls 3 Ns 's
OpenSSL backend
.Pq Pa src/io/tls_openssl.c
uses to detect that OpenSSL was selected at configure time via
.Fl -with-tls .
This file needs
.Em no
new configure flag: it is compiled unconditionally (like
.Pa src/os/os_rand.c ) ,
and its behavior follows whichever TLS backend the build already
chose.
.Sh NOSYS BEHAVIOR
When the build was configured with a TLS backend other than OpenSSL
(including
.Fl -with-tls Ns = Ns Cm none ) ,
.Dv XTC_TLS_BACKEND_OPENSSL
is undefined and every function below still
.Em links
(the public symbols are always present, exactly like
.Xr xtc_tls 3 Ns 's
.Pa tls_none.c
stub backend), but returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
at runtime after performing the same argument validation the real
implementation performs.  A caller can safely call these functions on
any build configuration and branch on the return value; only a
misconfigured or absent OpenSSL turns a would-be
.Dv XTC_OK
into
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS ,
never a link error.
.Sh HASHING
.Fn __os_crypto_sha256
writes the 32-byte SHA-256 digest of
.Fa data
.Bq Li 0.. Ns Fa len
to
.Fa out .
.Fn __os_crypto_hmac_sha256
writes the 32-byte HMAC-SHA256 of
.Fa data
under
.Fa key
to
.Fa out .
Both are intended for WAL-record and general checksum integrity, not
password storage (there is deliberately no HKDF or password-hashing
primitive in v1; see
.Sx DEFERRED ) .
.Pp
A
.Dv NULL
.Fa out ,
or a
.Dv NULL
.Fa data Ns / Ns Fa key
with a nonzero length, returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL .
An unexpected OpenSSL failure returns
.Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL
(an invariant violation, not an expected runtime condition).
.Sh AUTHENTICATED ENCRYPTION
.Fn __os_crypto_aes_gcm_encrypt
and
.Fn __os_crypto_aes_gcm_decrypt
are a one-shot AES-256-GCM AEAD pair.
.Fa key
is 32 bytes.
.Fa iv
is 12 bytes, the standard 96-bit GCM nonce length (also OpenSSL's
default IV length for
.Fn EVP_aes_256_gcm ,
so no
.Dv EVP_CTRL_AEAD_SET_IVLEN
call is needed).
.Fa aad
may be
.Dv NULL Ns / Ns Li 0
for no additional authenticated data.
.Fa tag
is 16 bytes.
.Pp
.Sy Reusing an
.Pq Fa key , Fa iv
.Sy pair for two different plaintexts is a catastrophic
confidentiality break.
Draw a fresh
.Fa iv
per encryption, e.g. from
.Fn __os_csprng_bytes .
.Pp
.Fn __os_crypto_aes_gcm_decrypt
verifies the GCM tag
.Em before
trusting the recovered bytes.  On a tag mismatch it returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
and does
.Em not
leave the original plaintext in
.Fa plaintext_out :
any bytes already written by the underlying decrypt-update step are
explicitly zeroed before returning, so a caller who forgets to check
the return value cannot accidentally observe unauthenticated data.
This is a code distinct from both
.Dv XTC_OK
and
.Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL
(an OpenSSL invariant violation unrelated to tag verification), so a
caller can never confuse "the ciphertext was tampered with" with
"something else went wrong."
.Sh CSPRNG
.Fn __os_csprng_init
allocates a per-handle CSPRNG object; the caller owns the handle and
must release it with
.Fn __os_csprng_destroy
(a
.Dv NULL
argument is a no-op, like
.Xr __os_alloc 3 Ns 's
.Fn __os_free ) .
.Fn __os_csprng_bytes
streams
.Fa len
cryptographically-strong pseudorandom bytes into
.Fa buf .
.Pp
.Sy Design note :
rather than hand-roll a second DRBG, the v1 implementation delegates
generation directly to OpenSSL's own default
.Fn RAND_bytes .
OpenSSL 3.x's default generator is already a properly-seeded,
continuously-reseeded, thread-safe CSPRNG (seeded from the OS entropy
source on first use); duplicating that machinery would add real
complexity (parameter plumbing, reseed policy, provider fetch/free
lifetime) for no security benefit in the stated v1 use case (per-loop
nonces/keys/jitter, not FIPS-mode determinism).
.Fn __os_csprng_init
mixes
.Fn __os_rand_u64
draws
.Pq see Pa src/inc/os_sharp.h ; Pa src/os/os_rand.c
into OpenSSL's entropy pool via
.Fn RAND_add
as
.Em additional
entropy, per the "reuse the existing primitive as the seed source, do
not invent a new one" discipline; it does not replace OpenSSL's own
seeding.
.Pp
This is a
.Em real
CSPRNG: two calls to
.Fn __os_csprng_bytes
are expected to differ, and there is deliberately no seed/replay
determinism contract (contrast with the DST seeded PRNG tree in
.Pa src/evt/sim.c Ns 's
.Fn __xtc_sim_rng ,
which
.Em is
required to replay identically for a given seed).  This primitive is
NOT wired into the deterministic-simulation
.Fn __xtc_sim_active
guard: it is meant for real per-loop nonce/jitter generation outside
the scheduler's replay-sensitive path, not for anything a DST run
observes.  A future task that makes the CSPRNG reachable from
sim-controlled code must add an
.Fn __xtc_sim_nondeterminism
call at the point of use, mirroring
.Pa src/os/os_time.c Ns 's
real-clock guard.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success,
.Dv XTC_OK
.Pq 0 .
On error, a negative
.Dv XTC_E_* :
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
(bad argument),
.Dv XTC_E_RANGE
(a length exceeds
.Dv INT_MAX ,
which OpenSSL's API takes as
.Vt int ) ,
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM
( Fn __os_csprng_init
allocation failure),
.Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL
(an OpenSSL invariant violation), or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
(OpenSSL is not the selected TLS backend; see
.Sx NOSYS BEHAVIOR ) .
.Fn __os_crypto_aes_gcm_decrypt
additionally returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
on a tag-verification failure (see
.Sx AUTHENTICATED ENCRYPTION ) .
.Fn __os_csprng_destroy
has no failure mode.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
All operations are safe to call from any thread.  OpenSSL's default
RNG (which
.Fn __os_csprng_bytes
delegates to) is internally thread-safe.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
uint8_t digest[XTC_CRYPTO_SHA256_LEN];
if (__os_crypto_sha256(record, record_len, digest) != XTC_OK)
        abort();          /* or XTC_E_NOSYS: OpenSSL not selected */

__os_csprng_t *rng = NULL;
uint8_t key[XTC_CRYPTO_AES_KEY_LEN], iv[XTC_CRYPTO_AES_IV_LEN];
if (__os_csprng_init(&rng) == XTC_OK) {
        (void)__os_csprng_bytes(rng, key, sizeof(key));
        (void)__os_csprng_bytes(rng, iv, sizeof(iv));
}

uint8_t tag[XTC_CRYPTO_AES_TAG_LEN];
uint8_t ciphertext[sizeof(plaintext)];
if (__os_crypto_aes_gcm_encrypt(key, iv, NULL, 0, plaintext,
    sizeof(plaintext), ciphertext, tag) != XTC_OK)
        abort();

uint8_t recovered[sizeof(plaintext)];
int rc = __os_crypto_aes_gcm_decrypt(key, iv, NULL, 0, ciphertext,
    sizeof(ciphertext), tag, recovered);
if (rc == XTC_E_INVAL)
        abort();          /* tampered: recovered is zeroed, not trusted */

__os_csprng_destroy(rng);
.Ed
.Sh DEFERRED
The following are deliberately
.Em not
in v1 and are tracked as future work, not oversights:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
ChaCha20-Poly1305 (a second AEAD cipher; AES-256-GCM alone covers the
stated use case).
.It
SHA-3 and BLAKE3 (a second and third hash family; SHA-256 alone
covers WAL/checksum integrity).
.It
HKDF (key derivation; no v1 consumer derives sub-keys yet).
.It
Alternative backends -- libsodium, BoringSSL, SChannel, CommonCrypto
(OpenSSL is already a build dependency for TLS; v1 does not add a
second cryptography dependency).
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr __os_alloc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_tls 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
.Sh HISTORY
First appeared in xtc 0.0.1, v1 scope (PLAN.md 19.2).
